      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-02T15:43:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "continue to dislike the distinction of a head node. there should be no difference between any of the compute nodes. what we call the \"head\" node is simply a compute node with some capability distributed to it.\n\nWhy is there a need to make a distinction here?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 24,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 24,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "e0fb763c9429ba35ca758001b04f03e961652866",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "55bbe2de_44ce26f5",
        "filename": "roles/compute-node-enable/tasks/main.yml",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 24,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000011
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-02T16:10:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "TL;DR: I agree with you, but making this happen would be a lot of work that goes beyond the scope of this changeset.\n\nThis problem is rooted in how MaaS is integrated.\n\nWhat\u0027s happening here is loading TOSCA files into XOS.  Ideally, the cycle would be:\n\n  1. Create/generate TOSCA on config system\n  2. Load TOSCA into XOS from the config system\n\nBut how the system currently works is\n\n  1. Create/generate TOSCA on config system\n  2. Copy config to head node\n  3. Copy XOS login/password information to head node\n  4. Load TOSCA into XOS from head node, using login/password info\n\nThe reason for this is that the MaaS compute node provisioner runs in a container on the head node, and thus has no access to the config or login/pw information. \n\nWhat this patch does is clean up variables that had different defaults settings (which throws errors when generating documentation), and change it so that you couldn\u0027t accidentally generate a password from within that provisioner container, which has been happened in some rare cases.\n\nI agree that this is a hack we want to get away from, but I don\u0027t see how we can do this prior to disintegrating MaaS from the provisioning system, or changing the way OpenStack compute nodes are added to XOS.",
      "parentUuid": "cef5786e_c4407c2c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 24,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 24,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "e0fb763c9429ba35ca758001b04f03e961652866",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d0639c4d_5dbaf6d3",
        "filename": "roles/compute-node-enable/tasks/main.yml",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 24,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000003
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-02T17:07:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The problem is that if we keep making changes that don\u0027t address the core issue then it will be harder to unwind and correct later. We are just incurring more technical debt and making it sufficiently complex to undo.\n\nThe provisioner should be using an ssh key to connect to the servers and thus it should not need credentials, so I am a bit confused as to the need.\n\nThe ssh-key is volume mounted into the container I believe.",
      "parentUuid": "55bbe2de_44ce26f5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 24,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 24,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "e0fb763c9429ba35ca758001b04f03e961652866",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "79667e10_f1431592",
        "filename": "roles/compute-node-enable/tasks/main.yml",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 24,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000011
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-01-02T18:06:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You\u0027re welcome to tackle that technical debt issue at any time.  :)\n\nThis isn\u0027t an SSH key issue - it has to do with XOS API endpoints and the passwords to access them - these are volume mounted in the docker container.\n\n`xos_admin_pass` is a path to where the password is generated when the configuration is created.\n\n`head_xos_admin_path` is the location where that password is copied to on the head node.  Previously there was a `maas_xos_admin_pass` that served a similar role.",
      "parentUuid": "d0639c4d_5dbaf6d3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 24,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 24,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "e0fb763c9429ba35ca758001b04f03e961652866",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
